  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 251 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The center of the low associated with the \nremnants of Isaias will track up into the Mid-Atlantic then \ntrack offshore before it reaches any farther north. Rainfall \nwill spread into southern areas this evening but struggle to \nmake it any farther north as it is running into the ~1030 MB \nhigh that is giving us the dry weekend. The 00Z guidance has \ntrended south with the northern extent of the rain, with the \nglobal deterministic data showing the most dramatic retreat \nwhile the HREF and global ensembles show a more subtle retreat. \nOverall, areas with a 50 percent or higher chance of seeing 0.1 \ninches is now mostly restricted to southern Vermont, with \nmeasurable rain likely not reaching the international border in \nplaces. Forecast rain amounts range from close to a half inch in\nsouthern Windsor/Rutland counties to nothing along much of \nQuebec border.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A surface low will pass through from west to \neast Tuesday night, bringing widespread showers. While it will \nbe quick moving and technically more northern stream and not \nhave a strong connection to Gulf/Atlantic moisture, it will \ninclude the remnants of Tropical Storm Rachael so there will be \nsome efficient rainfall with the former tropical moisture. \nGEFS/EPS/CMCE combined probabilities of a half inch of rain \nrange between 40-70 percent and probabilities of an inch are \nbetween 10-30 percent, so the rain would only be beneficial. The\nheaviest rain should fall in northern areas. Warm air aloft \nwill remain on the backside so even though the cold front drops \ntemperatures significantly, any upslope showers look to remain \nrain in the mountains. Conditions trend drier for Wednesday, but\nunder a classic cyclonic northwest flow cold air advection \nregime, there will be some upslope sprinkles and showers but no \nwidespread rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: A dynamic low pressure system brings winds and \nrain to the region for Friday into Saturday. Showers will linger\nbehind this system as upper level trough remains over the \nregion. Still a lot of uncertainty about this system since it is\nsix days away. Models are still not in very good agreement in \nthis timeframe either. GFS has a deeper and stronger low while \nthe ECMWF depicts more of an open wave as the system moves \nthrough.",
